export const KB = "# 00 \u00b7 FasciApp Body Assessment: report template\n\n**Reference:** Steven Kessler, *The 5 Personality Patterns* (2015): core rights and \"primary + backup safety strategy\" framing.\n\n**Internal knowledge base:** 01 body reading \u00b7 02 zones of fear \u00b7 03 belts \u00b7 04 character structures \u00b7 05 Anatomy Trains \u00b7 06 session planning \u00b7 07 trauma-informed.\n\n**Rules for writing:**\n- Warm, plain language, in our own words.\n- Never quote manuals word-for-word.\n- Every interpretation is a *possibility to explore* (\"may\", \"often\", \"invites\").\n- Strengths come first.\n- Keep it short enough to absorb (01: share only the key points).\n\n---\n\n## 0 \u00b7 Whole-being map (always first)\nA half-page synthesis that shows how all the layers meet in this one person, before any detail. Structure:\n1. **Three strengths you see** in the body and in the intake answers (MER / Myers \"3 good things\").\n2. **The core pattern in one sentence**, linking the layers. Example: \"A Leader-type pattern: energy lifted into the chest and head (character), held breath at the diaphragm (breath), a braced diaphragmatic and thoracic belt (belts), a forward pelvic shift with the Deep Front Line working hard (fascia), echoed by the low-back zone 'fear of losing' (emotion), and a nervous system that runs on high stress (trauma/regulation).\"\n3. **Small connection diagram** (text or table): Character \u2194 Face/body signs \u2194 Belt(s) \u2194 Fascial line(s) \u2194 Emotional theme / zone of fear \u2194 Breath pattern \u2194 Nervous-system state (window of tolerance).\n4. **Main direction for the 10 sessions** in one line. Example: \"From holding up to letting down: ground the legs, free the diaphragm, soften the chest.\"\n\n## 1 \u00b7 Reichian character structure (04)\n- Name each as \"Reichian name (BBTRS name) \u00b7 The right to \u2026\" (mapping table in 04), e.g. \"Psychopathic (Leader) \u00b7 The right to trust\".\n- Primary and secondary (backup) structures with confidence (low / medium / high) and the evidence for each, from the photos and intake.\n- Lead with each structure's **gift** and **life task**, then the body, breath and behavioural signs that fit.\n- \"Everyone is a mix\" disclaimer.\n\n## 2 \u00b7 Body reading: face and body (01, 02)\n- Positive qualities first.\n- Posture and alignment (front / side / back), breath, tension zones, face and eyes.\n- Body splits (front/back, left/right, top/bottom, head/body, torso/limbs).\n- **Zones of fear (02):** at most 3\u20134 zones that show in both the photos and the pain or tension map, phrased as questions.\n\n## 3 \u00b7 Belts of tension (03)\n- For each of the 7 belts: charge level (over / balanced / under), the evidence, and the \"basic right\" theme.\n- Note pairings (jaw\u2194pelvis, throat\u2194diaphragm).\n\n## 4 \u00b7 Fascial trains: Anatomy Trains (05)\n- Neutral skeletal description per view.\n- The lines that are probably involved, and the stations where they cross the pain areas.\n- Which line to release first.\n\n## 5 \u00b7 Emotional patterns (01, 02, 04, 07)\n- Emotional and physical patterns of the structure.\n- Breath pattern (collapsed vs. inflated chest).\n- Zones of fear from the intake, if not covered in 2.\n- Nervous-system / trauma state: where on the continuum, how wide the window of tolerance is, any dissociation cues.\n- Only use what the client chose to share in field 10.\n\n## 6 \u00b7 Ten-session treatment plan (06, 07)\nA table with 10 rows: **Session \u00b7 Focus \u00b7 Bodywork \u00b7 Breathwork \u00b7 Home practice**.\n- Begin from the default arc (06). Reorder it by the leading belt, the main line and the character.\n- Pace it using 07.\n- Respect the touch preferences.\n- Re-assess with photos at sessions 5 and 10.\n\n## Red flags / refer to a doctor (always present)\n- If any `red_flags` are ticked, or the health / medication answers or age show a contraindication (07: pregnancy, blood thinners, recent trauma or accident, unexplained weight loss, numbness or loss of strength, uncontrolled blood pressure, heart disease, epilepsy, severe asthma, osteoporosis, recent surgery or fracture, severe mental illness):\n  - Start the report with: \"Please get medical clearance before bodywork and breathwork.\"\n  - Mark the plan as provisional and remove deep work and intense breath.\n- Strong dissociation or acute distress \u2192 suggest a therapist alongside (MER07 10.5).\n\n## Disclaimer (always at the end)\n> This assessment is a somatic and educational reflection based on photos and your answers, drawn from Myofascial Energetic Release, BioDynamic Breathwork and Anatomy Trains perspectives. It is not a medical diagnosis or treatment and doesn't replace a doctor, physiotherapist or psychotherapist. Body-type and emotional links are possibilities to explore, not labels. Please consult a medical professional about pain, injury or health conditions.\n\n---\n\n## Intake field \u2192 report section map\nLive form: https://fasciapp.app/app/assessment.html (source `fasciapp-repo/app/assessment.html`). Data keys are in `data`; `red_flags` is stored in its own column.\n\nSection codes: **0** map \u00b7 **1** character \u00b7 **2** body reading / zones \u00b7 **3** belts \u00b7 **4** fascial lines \u00b7 **5** emotional \u00b7 **6** plan \u00b7 **RF** red flags.\n\n| Form field (key) | Sections | How it's used |\n|---|---|---|\n| Photos: front / side / back | 0,1,2,3,4 | Posture, splits, symmetry, chest shape, pelvic tilt and shift, rotations, face and eyes; primary evidence for character and lines |\n| client_name | \u2014 | Header only |\n| complaint (what brings you in) | 0,5,6 | Presenting theme. Each structure has a typical complaint: fear/anxiety (Dreamer), fatigue (Needy), defeat (Leader), tension (Endurer), \"no feelings\" (Perfectionist) |\n| goal (be able to do again) | 0,6 | Direction of the plan, sessions 9\u201310 |\n| pain_areas | 2,3,4,6 | Zones of fear (02), belt location, fascial line stations |\n| pain_intensity | 6,RF | Pressure and pace. High = titrate more |\n| pain_type (sharp, burning, radiating, tingling\u2026) | RF,4 | Radiating or tingling + numbness \u2192 refer. Otherwise line tracing |\n| tension_areas | 2,3,4 | Belts (jaw = oral, diaphragm = diaphragmatic\u2026), zones, lines |\n| tension_when (morning, stress, sitting\u2026) | 5,6 | \"Under stress\" \u2192 nervous-system and emotional link. \"After sitting\" \u2192 DFL / hip flexors, home practice |\n| started, onset (sudden/gradual) | 5,RF,6 | Sudden after an event \u2192 trauma lens (07), and RF if recent |\n| better / worse | 6 | Choose the modalities and home practice |\n| injuries (surgeries, scars) | 4,6,RF | Scar and fascial pulls on lines; recent surgery = RF |\n| age | RF,6 | Under 16 \u2192 no intense breath. Age affects pace |\n| height_cm, weight_kg | 1,4 | Body build (thin and long vs. compact and heavy) as weak character evidence. Handle sensitively, never as judgement |\n| dominant_hand | 2,4 | Left/right split; Spiral and Functional line asymmetry |\n| structural (scoliosis, leg length, flat feet, hypermobility) + structural_other | 1,4,6,RF | Scoliosis/asymmetry \u2192 Lateral/Spiral line, Dreamer clue. Flat feet \u2192 DFL. Hypermobility \u2192 gentle pressure and stability focus |\n| daily (desk, standing, driving, phone, physical, caring for kids) | 4,5,6 | Postural load on the lines; caretaking theme (Needy); home practice |\n| exercise | 1,4,6 | Over-training or performance (Perfectionist/Leader); line use |\n| sleep | 5,6,0 | Regulation state, the breath focus |\n| stress (0\u201310) | 5,0,6 | Width of the window of tolerance \u2192 pacing (07) |\n| breath (shallow, holding, chest, mouth, free) | 0,1,2,3,5,6 | Breath pattern (01), belts 4\u20136, character, breathwork dosage |\n| health (conditions, medication) | RF,6 | Contraindications (07) |\n| red_flags (pregnancy, blood thinners, recent trauma, weight loss, numbness) | RF | Any ticked \u2192 medical clearance first, provisional plan |\n| emotional (optional) | 5,1,0 | Emotional patterns, trauma lens. Use only what is shared |\n| touch (light/medium/deep, ask first, avoid areas) + touch_notes | 6 | Bodywork pressure, consent protocol, areas to skip |\n| consent | \u2014 | Must be true before saving or assessing |\n\n\n## Extra research sources\nAlso draw on 08-reich-lowen-research.md (Reich segments, Lowen types, Kessler patterns, cross-reference) and 09-anatomy-trains-research.md (lines, photo signs, evidence strength). Keep wording tentative: body typing has weak inter-rater reliability.\n\n## Field mapping addendum (v0.23.129): body experience & energetics\n- `data.body_experience` (client's own words, \"How do you experience your body?\") \u2192 quote or paraphrase it in the **holistic map** core statement; it is the client's subjective anchor.\n- `data.energetics` (practitioner markers, array): where charge sits (Legs, Lower body, Torso, Chest, Face & eyes), grounding (Well grounded, Weak grounding), core (Strong core, Weak core, Fragmentation in the core, Fragmentation outside the core, Proportionate distribution), energy & patterns (Collapsed shoulders, Lack of energy, Never collapses / always holds up, Finishes projects, Rarely finishes projects) \u2192 use in the **holistic map** links and in **body reading** (charge distribution, grounding, core/periphery), and as supporting (never deciding) evidence for character structure.\n- `data.energetics_notes` \u2192 practitioner nuance; weave into body reading.\n- Wording stays tentative: \"seems to\", \"may suggest\", \"the practitioner noticed\". E.g. charge high in chest/face with weak grounding may fit an upward-displaced pattern; fragmentation may relate to the schizoid \"right to exist\" theme. Never diagnose.\n\n## Field mapping addendum (v0.23.130): Patterns self-report\n- `data.patterns` = {question_id: 0\u20133} (ids s1\u2013s5, o1\u2013o5, p1\u2013p5, m1\u2013m5, r1\u2013r5; questions in 04-character-structures.md). `data.pattern_scores` = {schizoid, oral, psychopathic, masochistic, rigid}, each 0\u201315.\n- Use in the **Reichian character structure** section as one input beside body reading and energetics markers, never alone. Name the highest 1\u20132 as tentative (\"your answers lean toward\u2026, which the body reading partly supports/does not clearly show\"). Where self-report and body reading disagree, say so gently. No diagnosis.\n\n## Body map (required in every report, shown as the FIRST card)\n\nEvery new report MUST include `report.body_map` (it is stored in the `report` jsonb next to `sections`/`plan`; the card is hidden if missing):\n\n```json\n\"body_map\": {\n  \"belts\": {\"ocular\":0-3,\"oral\":0-3,\"cervical\":0-3,\"thoracic\":0-3,\"diaphragmatic\":0-3,\"abdominal\":0-3,\"pelvic\":0-3},\n  \"issues\": [{\"label\":\"Shoulders\",\"view\":\"front|back|both\",\"region\":\"shoulders\",\"text\":\"Possible ... (non-medical: possible, tendency)\"}],\n  \"note\": \"One gentle summary line\"\n}\n```\n\n- Belt levels: 0 balanced, 1 mild/to watch, 2 to watch, 3 strong/over-charged. Match the \"Belts of tension\" section.\n- Issue position: `region` key (eyes, jaw, neck_front, neck_back, shoulders, shoulder_l, shoulder_r, chest, upper_back, mid_back, diaphragm, belly, lower_back, pelvis, hips, knees, feet) or explicit `x`,`y` in the 200\u00d7460 figure. Numbers follow array order.\n- Only mark issues supported by the client's data; wording stays non-medical.\n\n\n# 01 \u00b7 Body reading (MER emotional body reading)\n\n**Source:** MER Module 07 *Trauma Release* (Satyarthi Peloquin / myofascialtrainings.com), ch. 7 \"Emotional Body Reading\", PDF pp. 35\u201342 (printed pp. 29\u201336). Paraphrased summary for internal use only; do not quote or republish.\n\n## Core idea (PDF p. 35\u201336)\n- Body shape comes from genes **and** life history. Repeated emotional experience gets \"set\" into posture: places that over-brace for defence (armour) and places that go under-used or weak (neglect).\n- Each person carries a \"body script\": the posture, tension and movement they learned was allowed. Strict upbringing can show as rigid control; shame or neglect can show as collapse or withdrawal.\n- Wording to remember: *structure reflects past function*. The way the body is built shows how it has been used and protected.\n- Lineage: Freud \u2192 Reich (muscle armour, 7 belts) \u2192 Pierrakos (body-reading method).\n\n## How to read (PDF pp. 37\u201339)\n**Attitude:**\n- The client should feel comfortable, not inspected. Talk as equals and don't stare.\n- Stay curious. Don't over-interpret. Notice what the body *expresses* and how it resonates in you.\n- **Always open with three positive qualities** you see, then ask the client what they notice in their own posture.\n- A reading is a starting point, not a list of things to fix. Share only the few observations that matter now, so the client isn't overloaded.\n- Reading also motivates: seeing their own story mirrored validates the client and shows them it can change.\n\n**Five things to observe:**\n1. **Posture and alignment.** Is weight even? Is one side favoured? Where is there collapse or bracing? Sagging shoulders or a rounded back suggest carried burden. An over-erect, tight back and neck suggest a need for control.\n2. **Breath.** Shallow or held breath often goes with fear, anxiety or suppression. Ask for a few deep breaths and see which areas don't move (chest, back, belly, sides).\n3. **Tension zones.** Common ones are the neck, shoulders, jaw and low back.\n4. **Movement and gesture.** Is it fluid or guarded? Free or hesitant?\n5. **Face and eyes.** Look at the jaw, forehead and around the eyes. Avoided eye contact can mean withdrawal. A tight jaw can mean held anger.\n\n## Breath patterns (PDF pp. 40\u201341)\n- **The diaphragm is the gateway for feeling.** Children learn to tighten it to shut down overwhelming feelings. Less fear and sadness gets through, but less joy and pleasure too.\n- **Collapsed or narrow chest (stuck on the exhale):**\n  - Signs: low charge and a withdrawn posture.\n  - Emotional pattern: hurt and insecurity, a tendency to depression, avoiding conflict, receiving more than giving, and held-back assertion.\n- **Inflated or over-developed chest (stuck on the inhale):**\n  - Signs: a tight belly and rigid diaphragm, cut off from the gut.\n  - Emotional pattern: a controlling stance, giving but finding it hard to receive, showing strength while hiding tenderness and tears.\n\n## Body splits (PDF pp. 41\u201342)\n| Split | What to compare | Possible meaning (a hypothesis, never a label) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Front / back | Open front vs. a braced back | Front = the self we show. Back = what we keep out of view: tension between the shoulder blades or in the low back as stored burden, fear, shame or anger. |\n| Left / right | Size, tone and height of each side | Right = doing, logic, yang. Left = feeling, receptivity, yin. Imbalance suggests conflict between action and feeling. |\n| Top / bottom | Upper body vs. pelvis and legs | Strong top with weak legs = living in the head or social role, poorly grounded. Strong legs with a tight top = grounded but restricted in expression. |\n| Head / body | Calm face vs. tense body | An intellectual or composed presentation that doesn't match what the body shows. |\n| Torso / limbs | Strong core with thin limbs, or the reverse | Strong core, weak limbs = energy bottled in, hard to act. Strong limbs, weak core = acting without connection to self. |\n\n## In photos\n- **Front photo:** left/right symmetry, head tilt, shoulder heights, chest open or collapsed, arms, knees, feet, face and eyes.\n- **Side photo:** head forward, chest inflated or collapsed, shape of the belly, pelvic tilt, knee lock, top/bottom and front/back balance.\n- **Back photo:** shoulder blades, spinal curves or twist, waist creases, glute tone, the heels.\n\n## Zone of fear\nThe 27 zones of fear (see 02) are read in this same pass and reported inside the body-reading section, as gentle \"possible themes\".\n\n\n# 02 \u00b7 The 27 zones of fear\n\n**Source:** MER Module 07 *Trauma Release*, section 9.1 \"27 Zones of Fear\", PDF pp. 47\u201349 (printed pp. 41\u201343; p. 49 is the body diagram). *The user calls this \"chapter 27\". In the manual it is section 9.1, under ch. 9 \"Practical application: techniques in MER\". There is no chapter 27. The name comes from the 27 zones.* Paraphrased.\n\n**How to use it:** the manual says these links are *clues to explore, not fixed labels*.\n- In a report, phrase each one as an invitation (\"tension here is sometimes linked with\u2026; does that resonate?\").\n- Report it inside **Body reading** (where the tension shows in the photos) or **Emotional patterns** (where it shows in the intake answers).\n- Name at most 3\u20134 zones, choosing those confirmed by both the photos and the pain or tension answers.\n\n| # | Zone | Theme (paraphrased) | Intake hook |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Feet | Fear of being oneself (identity, grounding) | pain: Feet; tension: Feet |\n| 2 | Lower legs / calves | Fear of acting or moving forward | Calves |\n| 3 | Knees | Fear of death or existential vulnerability | Knees |\n| 4 | Upper legs | Fear of one's own power, of being left behind | Thighs, Hamstrings |\n| 5 | Pelvis | Fear of fully living life | Pelvis, Hips |\n| 6 | Abdomen | Difficulty breathing in and taking in or digesting experience | Abdomen, Diaphragm |\n| 7 | Genitals / anus / pubis | Fear around sex and intimacy | (only if the client raises it) |\n| 8 | Sit bones / coccyx | Fear around keeping what's safe (resources, security) | Glutes, sitting pain |\n| 9 | Chest | Sadness, grief, sentimental sorrow | Chest |\n| 10 | Hands | Fear of making mistakes | Wrists/hands |\n| 11 | Forearms / elbows | Fear of punishment, of being \"less than\" | Elbows |\n| 12 | Upper arms | Discouragement | Arms |\n| 13 | Shoulders / upper back | Carrying the weight of the world | Shoulders, Upper back |\n| 14 | Mid and low back | Fear of losing (control, people, resources) | Lower back |\n| 15 | Back of the neck | Feeling disoriented or out of place | Neck |\n| 16 | Front of the neck | Guilt, unspoken words | Neck (front) |\n| 17 | Scalp | Worry, preoccupation (tension headaches) | Head |\n| 18 | Forehead | Perplexity, overthinking | Head; furrowed brow in the photo |\n| 19 | Eyebrows | Held anger or frustration | photo: brows |\n| 20 | Eye sockets | Prejudice, fixed judgement | photo: eyes |\n| 21 | Cheekbones | Shame | photo |\n| 22 | Nose | Fear of being controlled | photo |\n| 23 | Nose-to-mouth lines | Disappointment, unmet needs | photo |\n| 24 | Mouth / lips | Disgust, resistance | photo |\n| 25 | Chin | Feeling inferior (self-esteem) | photo |\n| 26 | Jaw / masseter | Revulsion, held anger | Jaw (pain or tension) |\n| 27 | Ears | Fear of not understanding or being misunderstood | photo / history |\n\n**Paired links worth noting:**\n- Zone 26 (jaw) pairs with the pelvic belt (03, jaw\u2013pelvis pairing).\n- Zones 13\u201314 overlap with the thoracic and abdominal belts.\n\n\n# 03 \u00b7 Reich's seven belts (segments) of tension\n\n**Sources (paraphrased):**\n- BBTRS Student Manual (Giten Tonkov): \"Belts of tension and the 7 segments\", PDF pp. 49\u201359 (printed 45\u201355); 8-session series PDF pp. 60\u2013100.\n- MER07 ch. 7.2.1 table \"The 7 belts of tension by Reich\", PDF pp. 36\u201338.\n\n## Principle\n- Armour is energy bound up in chronic muscle contraction. The bands run *across* the body's head-to-pelvis flow of life energy.\n- One belt is often **over-charged** (thick, tight, hot). The belts next to it can be **under-charged** (thin, low tone).\n- In BBTRS the belts are opened **from the top down**, so energy rising from the pelvis has a clear path.\n- Each session focuses on one belt and includes its neighbours.\n\n| Belt | Area | Held feelings (summary) | Signs to look for (photo / intake) | Image |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Ocular | Eyes, forehead, scalp, temples, occiput | Watchfulness, suspicion, fear, anger, grief, detachment | Fixed or wide stare, blank eyes, furrowed brow; headaches, migraine, eye strain, stress | blindfold |\n| 2 Oral | Mouth, chin, jaw, nose, ears | Longing, disgust, contempt, held anger, need for contact | Strong or receding jaw, tight lips; grinding, TMJ pain, tight \"pinched\" voice | gag |\n| 3 Cervical | Deep neck, tongue, SCM, upper trapezius | Unspoken words, self-pity, hurt, rage, helplessness | Neck pulled into the shoulders (\"shell\"), forward head; throat tightness, \"I wish I'd said\u2026\", trouble speaking up or talking non-stop | lasso |\n| 4 Thoracic (incl. arms) | Chest, shoulders, arms, shoulder blades, intercostals | Heart feelings: grief, love, longing, anger, joy | Chest armoured or collapsed, rounded or raised shoulders, arms held stiff; boundary and co-dependence themes, shut-down feeling | harness |\n| 5 Diaphragmatic | Diaphragm, solar plexus, stomach (psoas link) | Control, pain and pleasure, rage, terror, power | Ribs flared or held, waist \"split\", breath stuck high; anxiety, need for control, low vitality | corset |\n| 6 Abdominal | Rectus, transversus, obliques, low back (lats, QL) | Trust, nourishment, fear, pleasure | Belly held in, low-back tightness; gut complaints (IBS, constipation), numbness, addictions | \u2014 |\n| 7 Pelvic (incl. legs) | Pelvic floor, glutes, adductors, hamstrings, psoas/iliacus, lower legs | Sexuality, power, anger, panic, pleasure | Pelvis tucked or tipped, locked knees, thin or weak legs; heaviness or restlessness, ungrounded, spacey | chastity belt |\n\n## Pairings (PDF p. 58)\n- **Eyes, hands, feet:** contact with reality. Grounding work brings energy to the extremities (pushing with feet and hands, moving the eyes).\n- **Jaw and pelvis:** they lock and release together.\n- **Throat, ribcage and diaphragm:** they share speech and breath. Opening the throat can suddenly release the diaphragm, so pace it and keep the eyes open and grounded.\n\n## Chakra overlay (PDF p. 50)\nEach belt is matched to a \"basic right\":\n\n| Belt | Basic right |\n|---|---|\n| Ocular | to see |\n| Throat (oral and cervical) | to be seen and heard |\n| Heart (thoracic) | to be loved |\n| Solar plexus (diaphragmatic) | to act |\n| Sacral (abdominal) | to feel and want |\n| Root (pelvic) | to be here and to have |\n\n## Caution (PDF p. 60)\n- Use one or two activation exercises per session at most.\n- Never push a client into overcharge. Overwhelm disconnects them from felt sense.\n\n\n# 04 \u00b7 Reichian / bioenergetic character structures\n\n**Sources (paraphrased):** Steven Kessler, *The 5 Personality Patterns: Your Guide to Understanding Yourself and Others and Developing Emotional Maturity* (Bodhi Tree Press, 2015) for the pattern framing and core rights.\n**Main source:** BBTRS Character Structures Student Manual (Giten Tonkov). It draws on Lowen's *Bioenergetics* and Brennan's *Hands of Light*.\n- Pages are PDF pages: Dreamer 5\u201313, Needy 14\u201320, Leader 21\u201327, Endurer 28\u201334, Perfectionist 35\u201341, comparison table 42.\n- Illustrations are hanshoegaerts.be CC BY-NC-SA. Don't reuse them.\n\n**Rules:**\n- Everyone is a *mix*. Report one primary structure and one or two secondary ones, each with a confidence level.\n- Always lead with the **gift** and the **life task**. The manual stresses balancing honest work on shortcomings with support for the higher self (PDF p. 4).\n- Never present a structure as a diagnosis.\n\n## Naming used in reports (Reich \u00b7 BBTRS \u00b7 Kessler \u00b7 core right)\nAlways show as **\"Reichian name (BBTRS name) \u00b7 The right to \u2026\"**, e.g. \"Psychopathic (Leader) \u00b7 The right to trust\". Kessler's pattern name can follow in brackets.\n\n| Reich (original) | BBTRS name (Giten, verified in manual contents p. 2) | Kessler, *The 5 Personality Patterns* | Core right (theme) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Schizoid | The Unwanted Child / Dreamer | Leaving | The right to exist (Existence) |\n| Oral | The Needy structure | Merging | The right to our needs (Need) |\n| Psychopathic | The Leader | Aggressive | The right to trust (Trust) |\n| Masochistic | The Endurer | Enduring | The right to autonomy (Autonomy) |\n| Rigid | The Perfectionist | Rigid | The right to love and sexuality (Love) |\n\nKessler frames these as *safety strategies* used under distress: usually a primary pattern and one backup pattern, not fixed types. Use that language in reports.\n\n## 1 \u00b7 Schizoid (Dreamer) \u00b7 The right to exist. Gift: universal connection\n- **Theme:** the right to exist. Shift: \"I have no right to be here\" \u2192 \"I have the right to be\". Healing: \"I am safe, I am here.\"\n- **Origin:** before or at birth, or the first months.\n- **Body (photo):**\n  - Body looks assembled from pieces, often tall and thin.\n  - Asymmetry and splits (front/back, top/bottom, left/right), twist in the spine, one shoulder bigger, head tilted.\n  - Thin wrists, ankles and calves; weak joints; poor grounding.\n  - Vacant or far-away eyes, a large forehead.\n  - Tension rings round the body, with the main block at the base of the skull.\n- **Breath:** shallow, frozen thorax, stuck on the exhale; inhaling is hard.\n- **Physical patterns:** cold hands and feet, poor coordination, falls.\n  - Health themes: migraine, IBS, chronic fatigue, scoliosis, asthma or allergies, environmental sensitivities, fibromyalgia, anxiety or panic, dissociation.\n- **Emotional / behavioural:**\n  - Lives in the head, withdraws, intellectualises, takes pride in not needing others.\n  - Feels like a burden and doesn't belong.\n  - Suppressed rage and terror of falling apart.\n  - Defence: \"I'll reject you first.\"\n- **Intake hooks:** anxiety or panic, poor sleep, numbness or dissociation, high stress, structural asymmetry (scoliosis), cold extremities, \"Feels free\" rarely ticked.\n- **Therapy notes:**\n  - Small window of tolerance, so build trust first and resource a lot.\n  - Breathe into the body. Strengthen boundaries and grounding.\n  - Don't dwell on pain talk.\n  - Bodywork starts at the forehead, eyes and throat.\n  - Rage may surface only after trust is built.\n\n## 2 \u00b7 Oral (Needy) \u00b7 The right to our needs. Gift: nurturance\n- **Theme:** the right to have needs. Healing: \"I am enough.\"\n- **Origin:** first ~18 months; felt abandonment or \"not enough\" mothering; early pseudo-independence.\n- **Body:**\n  - Under-developed, long, slack muscles; collapsed or cold chest; slumped forward.\n  - Head and neck tension, chronic back and shoulder tension.\n  - Thin lips, chin jutting out or tucked in, clenched jaw, narrow throat, \"longing\" eyes.\n  - Pelvis low in charge.\n- **Breath:** shallow, sucking air in, belly tight against sobbing.\n- **Physical patterns:** fatigue, burnout, headaches, dizziness, recurring minor illness, eating issues, addictions, depression.\n- **Emotional:**\n  - Caretaker (\"I'll look after you\") or clingy.\n  - Disappointment (\"it's never enough\"), held-in anger over abandonment, fear that asking for needs means being left.\n- **Intake hooks:** fatigue, neck and shoulder pain, collapsed chest in the side photo, \"Caring for kids\" or caretaking roles, poor sleep.\n- **Therapy notes:**\n  - Validate when they name a need. Keep clear boundaries yourself.\n  - Breathe up into the collapsed chest. Strengthen the legs and feet.\n  - Practise reaching out with the arms (thoracic).\n  - Self-soothing, sunlight, simple pleasure.\n\n## 3 \u00b7 Psychopathic (Leader) \u00b7 The right to trust. Gift: courage\n- **Theme:** the right to trust. Healing: \"I can surrender; I'm perfectly imperfect.\"\n- **Origin:** ~8 months to 4 years; a seductive or using parent dynamic; betrayal.\n- **Body:**\n  - Inflated, \"blown-up\" upper body; head large and pushed forward.\n  - Intense, bright or dominating eyes.\n  - Tension at the eyes, shoulders and base of the skull, and from the chest to the waist.\n  - Split at the waist; arms held stiff and away from the body.\n  - Weak, ungrounded legs; under-charged pelvis.\n- **Breath:** hard to exhale (\"not allowed to give up\"); held in the upper chest.\n- **Physical patterns:** body signals noticed late; burnout; heart, hip, pelvic or prostate issues; addictions.\n- **Emotional:**\n  - Needs to be right and in control. Charming or overpowering.\n  - Craves intensity and recognition; skips completion; clashes with authority.\n  - Deep down feels unlovable.\n- **Intake hooks:** high stress with low sensed pain, chest breathing, low back or hip, ambitious \"goal\".\n- **Therapy notes:**\n  - Honest, competent presence with clear boundaries. The client decides about touch. Expect testing.\n  - Soften the upper body and bring energy down to the belly, pelvis and legs (kicking).\n  - Long exhales for letting go. Heart opening.\n\n## 4 \u00b7 Masochistic (Endurer) \u00b7 The right to autonomy. Gift: loyalty\n- **Theme:** the right to be free and to object. Healing: \"I am free, I am me.\"\n- **Origin:** ~2.5\u20134 years; controlling or sacrificing care; self-assertion shamed.\n- **Body:**\n  - Thick, heavy, compact muscles; short thick neck.\n  - Tight SCM and face, \"polite\" smile.\n  - Shoulders rolled forward, tailbone tucked.\n  - \"Pressure cooker\" between the neck and pelvis, choked at the neck and waist.\n  - Hot and cold areas.\n- **Breath:** contained, armoured chest, belly held.\n- **Physical patterns:** chronic tension (often the presenting complaint), endurance of pain, low-grade depression, overeating, anticipatory anxiety.\n- **Emotional:**\n  - Nice and compliant outside, resentful inside. Procrastination, complaining, passive resistance.\n  - Guilt around pleasure and success.\n- **Intake hooks:** \"tension\" is the main complaint, neck and shoulders, pelvis tucked in the side photo, high stress, long history.\n- **Therapy notes:**\n  - Don't rush, advise or cheerlead. Let them vent and find their own answers.\n  - Open the throat and cervical segment for expression.\n  - Experience moving out of freeze. Turn held anger into strength.\n\n## 5 \u00b7 Rigid (Perfectionist) \u00b7 The right to love and sexuality. Gift: elegance\n- **Theme:** the right to love. Healing: \"I am real.\"\n- **Origin:** ~3\u20137 years; love felt rejected; feelings controlled.\n- **Body:**\n  - Proportionate, symmetrical, athletic.\n  - Head high, straight back, stiff neck, square determined jaw.\n  - Pelvis tipped back and \"cold\".\n  - Plate or mesh armour; energy in the eyes, hands and long muscles; diaphragm block.\n  - Two subtypes: romantic (open heart, muted sexuality) or sexual (closed heart, sexual expression).\n- **Breath:** controlled, shallow, hard to let go.\n- **Physical patterns:** muscle spasms, arthritis, ulcers, heart risk, throat issues, workaholism or burnout.\n- **Emotional:**\n  - High achiever with an inner critic; image matters; can't ask for help; fears vulnerability; \"yes, but\u2026\".\n- **Intake hooks:** very fit, \"Sport 5\u00d7/week\", stiffness more than pain, controlled breath, desk plus training.\n- **Therapy notes:**\n  - Release the diaphragm to reconnect heart and pelvis.\n  - Use the body as a vessel for feeling, not for performance.\n  - Expect anxiety when control loosens. Resource often.\n\n## Quick comparison (PDF p. 42, paraphrased)\n| | Dreamer | Needy | Leader | Endurer | Perfectionist |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Eyes | vacant | longing or puppy | penetrating or seductive | suffering, confused | bright but hard |\n| Sitting | collapsed, curled up | leans on the arms | leans forward, arms crossed | squirms on a hard seat | perfectly upright |\n| Closeness | avoids it | on the basis of need | only if in control | submissive | guarded but close |\n\n## Reading from 3 photos\n- **Front:** symmetry (Dreamer = asymmetric; Perfectionist = symmetric), the eyes, jaw, chest and legs.\n- **Side:** upper-body inflation (Leader), collapsed chest (Needy), compression and tucked tail (Endurer), upright with pelvis tipped back (Perfectionist), twist or dis-integration (Dreamer).\n- **Back:** shoulder asymmetry and twist (Dreamer), heavy, compressed back and neck (Endurer), narrow, weak legs (Leader, Needy).\n\n## Patterns self-report questions (intake v0.23.130)\nRated 0 Not at all \u00b7 1 A little \u00b7 2 Often \u00b7 3 Very much; sum per type 0\u201315. Client sees them interleaved without type names.\n\n**Schizoid** (s)\n1. When you're stressed, do you tend to go blank, numb or \"leave\" your body?\n2. Do you feel safer when you're alone or in your own head than with people?\n3. Do you often feel you don't quite belong anywhere?\n4. Do your hands and feet often feel cold, or do you feel cut off from your body?\n5. Do you find it hard to feel grounded or present in your legs?\n\n**Oral** (o)\n1. Do you find it hard to ask for help, or feel needy when you do?\n2. Do you run out of energy quickly and need others to recharge you?\n3. Do you fear being left or abandoned?\n4. Do you often feel you never get enough, whether love, food or rest?\n5. Do you give a lot to others, hoping to get care back?\n\n**Psychopathic** (p)\n1. Is it important for you to stay in control of situations?\n2. Do you find it hard to trust others or to show weakness?\n3. Do you feel you have to be strong or on top so you don't get used?\n4. Do you tend to lead, push or charm to get things done?\n5. Is it hard for you to admit you were wrong or to lose?\n\n**Masochistic** (m)\n1. Do you keep going and endure, even when you're exhausted?\n2. Do you hold in anger or frustration instead of showing it?\n3. Do you often feel stuck, heavy or under pressure?\n4. Do you find it hard to say no, then feel resentful later?\n5. Do you put off decisions or projects, then struggle to finish them?\n\n**Rigid** (r)\n1. Do you need things to be done right or perfectly?\n2. Is it hard for you to soften and show tender feelings?\n3. Do you push hard at work and find it difficult to fully relax?\n4. Do you fear being rejected if you open your heart?\n5. Do you keep your chest and back held straight and controlled?\n\n\n# 05 \u00b7 Anatomy Trains myofascial lines (Thomas Myers)\n\n**Sources (web research, paraphrased):**\n- Myers T., *Anatomy Trains*, 4th ed. (Elsevier 2020), ch. on \"BodyReading / Structural analysis\": https://basicmedicalkey.com/structural-analysis/\n- Anatomy Trains official site and BodyReading course outline: https://www.anatomytrains.com/product/bodyreading-visual-assessment-of-the-anatomy-trains-webinar-series/\n- ABMP CE, Myers \"BodyReading the Myofascial Meridians\": https://www.abmp.com/learn/course/bodyreading-myofascial-meridians-3-superficial-back-line\n- Line \"stations\" summary: https://www.sportsrehabexpert.com/public/472.cfm\n- Myers, \"Visual assessment of postural antecedents to nonspecific low back pain\" (CRC Press): https://doi.org/10.1201/9781032675886-19\n\n## Myers' method (applied to photos)\n1. **Describe the skeleton first,** in neutral words: tilt, shift, bend, rotation of the head, ribcage, pelvis, knees and feet, left vs. right. Compare front, side and back.\n2. **Ask which soft tissue could be holding that position,** then which line it belongs to. Lines can be partly involved.\n3. **Decide what to release first,** and in what order, so the work holds.\n4. Myers also starts with \"3 good things\" before naming any problem. This matches MER (see 01).\n\n| Line | Track (main stations) | Photo signs of restriction / shortness | Best view |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Superficial Back Line (SBL)** | Plantar fascia \u2192 calves \u2192 hamstrings \u2192 sacrotuberous lig. \u2192 erectors \u2192 scalp to the brow | Knees locked back (hyperextended), posterior pelvic tilt **or** exaggerated lumbar curve, hamstring tightness (poor roll-down), flattened or over-curved spine, head forward with a short sub-occiput, chin lifted | Side, back |\n| **Superficial Front Line (SFL)** | Toe extensors and tibialis anterior \u2192 quads and patella \u2192 rectus abdominis \u2192 sternal fascia \u2192 SCM | Startle-pattern posture: head forward, chest pulled down or collapsed, ribs pulled toward the pubis, flexed hips, knees locked or hyperextended | Side, front |\n| **Lateral Line (LL)** | Peroneals \u2192 ITB, TFL and glutes \u2192 obliques and QL \u2192 intercostals \u2192 SCM and splenius | Side-bend: one shoulder or hip higher, waist crease deeper on one side, ribcage shifted sideways, ankles rolled in or out (in- or eversion), knock knees or bow legs | Front, back |\n| **Spiral Line (SPL)** | Splenius \u2192 opposite rhomboids \u2192 serratus anterior \u2192 obliques \u2192 TFL/ITB \u2192 tibialis anterior \u2192 peroneus longus \u2192 biceps femoris \u2192 erectors (wraps the body twice) | Rotations: head turned or tilted, one shoulder forward, ribcage rotated on the pelvis, one knee rotated in, one arch fallen more than the other, scapula sitting further from the spine on one side | Front, back |\n| **Deep Front Line (DFL)** | Deep foot muscles and tibialis posterior \u2192 adductors \u2192 psoas and iliacus \u2192 front of the spine \u2192 diaphragm \u2192 pericardium \u2192 scalenes and jaw floor | The \"core\" line. Anterior pelvic tilt or forward pelvic shift, ribcage shifted back over the pelvis, lumbar compensation, collapsed inner arches, knock knees, shallow or held breath, forward head with short scalenes. Links to the diaphragm and psoas (see belts 5\u20137) | Side, front |\n| **Arm lines** (Superficial and Deep Front Arm; Superficial and Deep Back Arm) | Pec major / pec minor, biceps, flexors; trapezius, deltoid, extensors; rhomboids, rotator cuff, triceps | Rounded or protracted shoulders (front arm lines, pec minor), raised shoulders (back arm line, trapezius), arms rotated inward with the backs of the hands facing forward, elbows held bent, winged or uneven shoulder blades | Front, back |\n| **Functional lines** (Front, Back, Ipsilateral) | Lat \u2192 thoracolumbar fascia \u2192 opposite glute max \u2192 vastus lateralis; pec major \u2192 rectus sheath \u2192 opposite adductor longus | Mostly seen in movement, less in standing. Diagonal shoulder-to-opposite-hip asymmetry, one shoulder dropped with the opposite hip high, sports one-sidedness. Combine with dominant hand and sport answers | Back, front |\n\n## Bridging to MER / BBTRS\n| Anatomy Trains line | Related belt(s) | Related emotional theme |\n|---|---|---|\n| SFL (protective curl forward, startle) | thoracic, cervical | fear, protection of the heart |\n| SBL | ocular / occiput, back | holding up, the back as the \"unseen\" side |\n| DFL | diaphragmatic, abdominal, pelvic | core safety, breath, the psoas fear response |\n| Lateral and Spiral lines | \u2014 | left/right and twist patterns, body splits (01); Dreamer asymmetry (04) |\n| Arm lines | thoracic belt | reaching out, holding, pushing away (boundaries) |\n\nKeep these links as interpretive bridges, not anatomy claims.\n\n## Report wording\n- Write: \"Your side photo suggests the Deep Front Line may be working hard (pelvis forward, ribs back).\"\n- Don't write \"your DFL is shortened\". We infer from photos; we don't measure.\n\n\n# 06 \u00b7 Planning a 10-session bodywork + breathwork arc\n\n**Sources (paraphrased):**\n- BBTRS Student Manual: 8-session series, PDF pp. 60\u2013100; session 1 general assessment, PDF pp. 65\u201366; pairing of segments, p. 58.\n- MER Basic Student Handout: 5 steps toward pain-free living, the 6 guiding principles (via `mer-manuals/NOTES.md`, pp. ~8, 15\u201316).\n- MER07 ch. 8\u201310: trauma safety.\n- 07-trauma-informed.md.\n\n## Principles\n1. **Order of belts:** BBTRS works top-down (eyes \u2192 jaw/throat \u2192 chest \u2192 diaphragm/belly \u2192 pelvis). This clears a path for energy rising from the pelvis. The manual suggests 8\u201310 sessions in that order, and allows changing the order to fit the client's holding pattern. Use **TRE / gentle tremoring** early to wake the pelvis.\n2. **Always include the neighbouring belts** and the pairings (jaw\u2194pelvis, throat\u2194diaphragm, eyes/hands/feet = grounding).\n3. **MER 5 steps as the outer arc:**\n   - release spasm and pain\n   - restore alignment along the fascial lines\n   - awareness of the causes of stress\n   - re-educate movement\n   - home practice (stretches, self-massage)\n4. **MER principles in every session:**\n   - the client is a pro-active participant (feedback on pain and pressure)\n   - presence\n   - go slow\n   - hands on\u2013off (pendulation)\n   - no agenda\n   - balance of yin and yang\n5. **Trauma pacing:**\n   - Session 1 is resourcing only.\n   - Increase charge only after good integration.\n   - One or two activation exercises per session at most.\n   - Nose breathing to calm, mouth breathing to charge.\n6. **Character-specific adjustments (04):**\n   - Dreamer: slow, grounding, short charge.\n   - Needy: chest and legs, reaching out.\n   - Leader: from top to bottom, long exhales, client-led touch.\n   - Endurer: throat and expression, no rush.\n   - Perfectionist: diaphragm, heart\u2013pelvis, permission to lose control.\n7. **Fascial line priority (05):** release the most restricted line where it crosses the session's belt. Examples: the DFL with the diaphragm and pelvis sessions; the SFL and arm lines with the thoracic session; the SBL and occiput with the ocular session.\n8. **Each session** has four parts:\n   - **Focus** (belt + line + theme)\n   - **Bodywork** (MER strokes and holds, at the pressure the client chose in \"Touch\")\n   - **Breathwork** (calming \u2192 connected \u2192 charged, as tolerated)\n   - **Home practice** (one simple thing)\n9. **Re-assess** with the 3 photos at session 5 and session 10.\n\n## Default arc (adapt to the reading)\n| # | Focus | Bodywork | Breathwork | Home practice |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Assessment, safety, resources; whole-body | Gentle peripheral touch (hands, feet), rocking, joint shaking; agree on pressure and consent | Breath awareness, slow nose breathing, find a resource; short TRE | Resource list (inner/outer), 5 min nose breathing daily |\n| 2 | Ocular belt + occiput (SBL top) | Forehead, temples, around the eyes, sub-occipital release, ears | Eyes-open connected breath, eye movements, peripheral vision | Eye \"palming\", gaze to the horizon, an evening screen break |\n| 3 | Oral belt: jaw (pairs with pelvis) | Masseter, temporalis, floor of the mouth, TMJ holds | Jaw-loose mouth breathing, sighs, sound | Jaw release with tongue rest, yawning |\n| 4 | Cervical belt / throat (SFL top, scalenes = DFL) | SCM (carefully), trapezius, thoracic inlet, hyoid area | Breath with voice (humming, \"ahh\"), lion-style expression if resourced | Humming 3 min, neck mobility |\n| 5 | Thoracic belt + arm lines (re-assess photos) | Pecs and pec minor, intercostals, shoulder joint release, scapulae | Connected breath into the chest and back ribs; press on the exhale | Doorway chest opener, reaching-out arm movement |\n| 6 | Diaphragmatic belt (DFL core) | Rib springing on the exhale, diaphragm attachments under the ribs, solar plexus | Connected breath to the low ribs and belly; pendulate | Rib-squeeze breath, gentle belly self-massage |\n| 7 | Abdominal belt + low back (DFL / LL, QL) | Belly (only with trust), psoas approach, QL, thoracolumbar fascia | Belly breath, a longer charge if safe | Clockwise belly massage, child's pose breathing |\n| 8 | Pelvic belt + legs (DFL / SBL / LL) | Psoas and iliacus, glutes, adductors, hamstrings, feet | Pelvic-floor breath wave, TRE, grounding into the feet | Pelvic tilts with breath, barefoot walking |\n| 9 | Integration: the client's main line and belt | Long fascial strokes along the whole primary line(s), unwinding | Full connected breath, longer session if integrated | Personal 10-min routine |\n| 10 | Completion, re-assessment, self-care plan | Whole-body integration, grounding holds | Calm breath, closing meditation (healing sounds) | Ongoing plan + FasciApp practices |\n\n**When to change the order:**\n- Pain or tension is mainly low (feet, knees, pelvis) and the client is poorly grounded \u2192 bring sessions 8 and 2 forward.\n- Strong jaw or neck tension \u2192 sessions 3\u20134 earlier.\n- Small window of tolerance \u2192 repeat session 1-type resourcing before sessions 6\u20138.\n\n## Additions from the MER Trauma Release manual (TRM = Dropbox `/TRAUMA_RELEASE_MANUAL_updated_2.pdf`)\n- **Three key segments for trauma (TRM pp. 15, 33\u201344):** the MER trauma module focuses on oral/cervical, diaphragmatic and pelvic.\n  - **Neck work runs through all ten sessions** in layers, from superficial to deep intrinsic muscles and the occiput\u2013atlas junction (TRM p. 33).\n  - Opening the deep neck lets control go and energy spread down the body.\n  - In the default arc, add a short deep-neck or sub-occipital piece to every session, and give extra weight to sessions 3\u20134 (oral/cervical), 6 (diaphragm) and 8 (pelvis).\n- **Stroke families to draw on (TRM pp. 33\u201344):**\n  - **Oral/cervical:** neck layers C7\u2192occiput, neck decompression, SCM (clavicle and mastoid ends, gently), anterior and middle scalenes with head-turn stretch, scalp fascia, temporalis, sphenoid hold, masseter (with mouth-opening stretch), mandible attachments, zygomatic arch, frontalis.\n    - Inside-the-mouth pterygoid work only with a glove and explicit consent.\n    - After working one side, stand the client up to compare.\n  - **Diaphragmatic:** rib-cage stretch.\n  - **Pelvic:** see the TRM strokes manual.\n- **Bodywork + movement together (TRM pp. 23\u201325):**\n  - Pain is usually the result of posture and imbalance, not the source (except after recent trauma such as an accident).\n  - Pair manual release (decompress, free adhesions) with movement that strengthens and stabilises: postural therapy (Egoscue), yoga, Pilates, unwinding, TRE.\n  - Every home practice should include one movement or postural exercise, e.g. for head-forward / \"iNeck\" posture.\n- **Regulation-first pacing (TRM pp. 17, 26):**\n  - Before sessions 1 and 2, decide if the client leans **hyper** (calm down first) or **hypo** (gently bring charge back).\n  - Give over-aroused clients the Rosenberg eye-movement exercise as home practice from session 1, and sub-occipital balancing at the start of sessions.\n- **TRE (TRM pp. 21\u201322):** introduce it in session 1 at client-controlled intensity, then use it as home practice. It particularly supports the DFL and pelvic sessions (7\u20138).\n- **Emotional process (TRM pp. 28\u201329):** in every session, acknowledge small emotional signs, support defences, and ask body-based meaning questions. Don't analyse or fix.\n\n\n# 07 \u00b7 Trauma-informed practice (safety, pacing, titration)\n\n**Trauma manual used:**\n- **BBTRS Student Manual** (Giten Tonkov, 150 pp., `/workspace/brand/bai/bbtrs-manuals/BBTRS-Student-Manual-Giten-Tonkov.pdf`), trauma parts:\n  - Basic principles: PDF pp. 15\u201320\n  - Contraindications: PDF p. 12\n  - About trauma / nervous system: PDF pp. 36\u201343\n  - About release: PDF pp. 44\u201348\n  - 8-session cautions: PDF p. 60\n- **MER07 Trauma Release** (`/workspace/brand/bai/mer-manuals/MER07_Trauma-Release.pdf`):\n  - Ch. 1 (trauma continuum, PTSD / C-PTSD, PDF ~pp. 7\u20138)\n  - Ch. 8 (resourcing, dissociation, re-traumatisation, PDF pp. 43\u201346)\n  - Ch. 10 (post-session care, consent, language, referrals, contraindications, PDF pp. 66\u201370)\n\nAll paraphrased.\n\n- **MER Trauma Release training manual (Nisarga Eryk Dobosz / Integral Body Institute)**, Dropbox `/TRAUMA_RELEASE_MANUAL_updated_2.pdf`, 46 PDF pages. Cited below as **TRM p. X** (PDF page). This is the user's own course manual and the primary trauma source.\n\n## Core frame\n- **Trauma lives in the nervous system, not in the event** (BBTRS p. 39).\n- An incomplete fight/flight or freeze response stays in the tissues and forms the belts of tension (p. 38).\n- Release often comes as trembling or shaking, then a spontaneous shift from tight to deep breathing (p. 45). Let it complete.\n- **Continuum** (MER07 ch. 1): from coping well, to mild stress, to strained and relying on poor coping, to needing extra support or being at risk. Match intensity to where the client is.\n\n## Principles (BBTRS pp. 15\u201320; MER07 8.3)\n| Principle | In practice |\n|---|---|\n| **Felt sense** | Physical sensation without a story: \"what, where, what texture\". Separate the sensation from the emotional charge. |\n| **Resources** | List internal resources (strength, faith, humour) and external ones (nature, pets, music). Return to them often. |\n| **Pendulation** | Move between the charged area and the resourced area: touch, then pause, then movement and integration. |\n| **Titration** | Small doses. Light pressure that increases only as tissue softens. Discharge without full re-living. |\n| **Resonance** | The practitioner stays present, curious and non-knowing. \"No resistant client, only an inflexible therapist.\" |\n| **Check-ins** | Ask about pressure, sensations and emotions regularly. Pauses let the client integrate. |\n| **Consent** | Ask before touch and before each new area. The belly and centre come only after trust is built on the periphery (BBTRS p. 66). The client can say stop at any time. |\n\n## Signs of overwhelm or dissociation (MER07 8.2.3; BBTRS p. 44)\n- Numb or \"not there\", trouble naming sensations.\n- Glazed eyes or avoided eye contact; slower or vaguer speech; \"checked out\".\n- Fear in the eyes, shallow fast breath, panic.\n- Bracing, curling in, freezing.\n- Overcharge: fast, repetitive movement, losing contact.\n\n**Response:**\n1. Slow down and **breathe through the nose** (it calms the system).\n2. Open the eyes and orient to the room. Feel the feet and the support underneath.\n3. Name a resource. Lighten or stop touch.\n4. Resume mouth breathing (charge) only when the client is back.\n\n## Safety and limits \u2192 feed the red-flags note\n**Breathwork (BBTRS p. 12) \u2014 avoid or get clearance for:**\n- Pregnancy, especially the 1st trimester\n- Severe asthma, severe heart disease, epilepsy or seizures\n- Severe mental illness (psychosis, schizophrenia), acute injury, severe diabetes\n- Intoxication\n- Under 16 for intense techniques\n\n**Deep bodywork (MER07 10.5) \u2014 avoid or get clearance for:**\n- Osteoporosis, recent surgery or fracture, clotting disorders or blood thinners\n- Uncontrolled hypertension, varicose veins, heart disease, rheumatoid arthritis\n- Pregnancy (1st trimester), untreated bipolar disorder or schizophrenia, severe addiction, intoxication\n\n**Strong dissociation:** stabilise first with talk therapy or Somatic Experiencing, then bodywork.\n\n## After the session (MER07 ch. 10; BBTRS pp. 9\u201311, 66)\n- Integration continues for days. Water, rest, no big decisions straight away.\n- Grounding: barefoot walking, slow nose breathing, journaling.\n- The practitioner offers a check-in channel.\n\n## How this shapes the 10-session plan (see 06)\n- Session 1 is always assessment, resourcing and safety. No deep release.\n- Intensity rises only if the previous session integrated well. A small window of tolerance (Dreamer, high stress, \"Recent trauma\") means a slower arc and more resourcing sessions.\n- Every session ends with grounding and home practice.\n- If red flags are ticked \u2192 doctor first; plan is provisional.\n\n---\n\n## From the MER Trauma Release manual (TRM), paraphrased\n\n### Definitions and the trauma\u2013stress continuum (TRM pp. 15\u201316)\n- **Trauma:** anything that overwhelms a person's capacity to cope and is felt as life-threatening, leading to overwhelm and freeze. (The word means \"wound\".)\n- **Stress:** strain from hard inner or outer circumstances. Stress and trauma sit on one continuum.\n- **Types to ask about in the intake** (fields \"started\", \"onset\", \"emotional\"):\n  - innocent or developmental trauma\n  - physical (accidents, injuries)\n  - medical (surgery)\n  - exposure to violence\n  - chronic stress\n- **Practitioner risk:** vicarious traumatisation (absorbing clients' trauma) and emotional denial.\n\n### Anatomy of trauma (TRM p. 16)\n- Protective contraction centres on the **psoas and diaphragm**. That cuts blood and oxygen flow, which leads to low energy and pain.\n- A response meant to be temporary becomes a lifestyle, a chronic state of emergency, ending in fragmentation.\n- **Signs:**\n  - restricted breath (to feel less)\n  - numbness and loss of felt sense\n  - reduced energy to the hands and feet\n- **In the report:**\n  - Held breath + psoas/diaphragm tension (belts 5\u20137, DFL) + cold or numb extremities = a likely trauma-holding signature.\n  - Name it gently in section 5 and the whole-being map.\n\n### Two nervous-system states (TRM p. 17), which set the pacing\n| | Hyper-arousal (fight/flight) | Hypo-arousal (freeze/collapse) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Body | Raised heart rate, blood pressure and breath; high muscle tone; vigilance | Low heart rate and blood pressure; held breath; flaccid or numb muscles; fainting |\n| Mind / emotion | Fear, terror, aggression, racing thoughts | Compliant, dissociated, foggy; defeat |\n| Intake clues | Stress 7\u201310, poor sleep, \"Mouth/Chest breathing\", anxiety | \"Hold my breath\", numbness, low energy, flat affect, \"nothing happens\" |\n| Approach | Calm first: nose breath, slow strokes, longer exhales, grounding | Gently bring charge back: orient, movement, warmth, eyes open, small charge doses |\n| Safety | Over-arousal can tip into rage | Deep collapse needs care: screen for despair or suicidality \u2192 refer |\n\nA chronic emergency state creates a **new biological baseline**. Small stressors then trigger big reactions, and the long-term result can be hypertension or depression (TRM p. 17).\n\n### Resolution (TRM pp. 15, 17\u201318)\n- **Goal:** rebalance the autonomic nervous system (sympathetic = gas pedal, parasympathetic = brake).\n- **Frameworks:** polyvagal theory (social engagement), and bottom-up work through the body (tremors start in the brainstem and spinal cord). The psoas is the fight/flight muscle.\n- **Healing sequence:**\n  1. The body returns to relaxation through bodywork, unwinding, shaking and self-regulation.\n  2. Breath regains its natural volume and rhythm.\n  3. Felt sense returns.\n  4. Held charge discharges.\n  5. *Only then* is the story linked to the felt sense.\n- **Post-traumatic growth:** resolved trauma leaves the person stronger, without a need for revenge, shame or blame.\n- **Resources:**\n  - external: nature, friends, animals, spiritual practice, dance, a hot bath\n  - internal: strength, agility, talents, intelligence, positive felt sense\n  - plus grounding, centring and TRE\n\n### TRE: tension/trauma release exercises (TRM pp. 21\u201322)\n- Developed by David Berceli. Seven exercises that switch on the natural **neurogenic tremor** reflex, which mammals use to return to balance.\n- TRE works physically without going into \"the story\", though emotions may release.\n- **The client controls the amount of shaking**, so no overwhelm and safety throughout.\n- It mainly engages the **Deep Front Line**. Clients who practise TRE tremor more easily on the table, which allows deeper work.\n- TRE combines well with unwinding.\n- Use: a short TRE in session 1 and as home practice from about session 2 (if no red flags).\n\n### Vagus / social-engagement reset (TRM pp. 26\u201327, after Stanley Rosenberg)\n- Different tension on the left and right of the tissue under the base of the skull suggests an alert or freeze state. Gentle sub-occipital massage can even it out and shift the client toward rest.\n- **Home exercise for over-arousal (Rosenberg \"basic exercise\"):**\n  1. Lie on your back with fingers interlaced behind the head.\n  2. Keep the head still and look to one side with the eyes only, up to 30\u201360 s, until a sigh, yawn or swallow comes.\n  3. Repeat on the other side.\n- Good for high stress or poor sleep.\n\n### Working with emotions and defences (TRM pp. 28\u201329)\n- Notice small signs of feeling: a voice change, tears, a breath change, a sigh, a facial shift. Acknowledge them quietly. Ignoring them confirms \"my feelings don't matter\", and the structure slips back.\n- **Don't problem-solve.** The practitioner is not the psychologist. Feelings are there to be felt, accepted and understood.\n- **Support the defence, don't fight it.** It helped the client survive. Treat it as a resource, and the client learns to trust their own regulation.\n- **Go for meaning in the present.** Ask body-based questions, e.g. \"what is your hand saying?\", or what curling up and making the chest small expresses.\n- The practitioner's comfort with their own emotions is the best container.\n\n### Reichian body types, quick lens (TRM pp. 30\u201331)\nThese complement 04.\n\n| Type | Holding pattern | Central emotion | Basic doubt | Healing direction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Schizoid / Dreamer | Holds together | fear | right to exist | grounding, embodiment |\n| Oral / Needy | Holds on | sadness | right to have | find the core, stand on your own feet |\n| Masochist / Endurer | Holds in | resentment, guilt | right to act | turn resistance into action, saying \"No\" |\n| Psychopath / Leader | Holds up | anger | right to love | trust in something bigger |\n| Rigid / Perfectionist | Holds back | shame | right to want | own needs, feelings, shadow |\n\nNote: the ages of wounding differ slightly from the BBTRS manual. Use these as approximate ranges only.\n\n### Body parts (TRM pp. 3\u201314, used in 01 body reading)\nBefore reading, place yourself diagonally, or stand behind the client and look in the mirror together, so they don't feel confronted (TRM p. 3).\n\nFoot patterns with photo cues (TRM p. 5):\n- **Everted feet:** unclear direction.\n- **Inverted feet:** shyness, a weak right to be.\n- **Flat feet:** collapse and slow response. Often paired with an over-built back, neck and head. *Links to the DFL.*\n- **Clutching toes:** gripping for control.\n\n\n# 08 \u00b7 Reich, Lowen, Kessler, Hakomi: web research (paraphrased)\n\nSupplements 04-character-structures.md (BBTRS/Giten). Use the BBTRS manual as the primary source; this file adds outside sources and cross-checks. Always present findings as **tendencies and hypotheses, not diagnoses**.\n\n## Sources\n1. Reich W., *Character Analysis* (1933; 3rd enl. ed. 1949, FSG). Archive: https://archive.org/details/characteranalysi00reic\n2. Lowen A., *The Language of the Body* (1958) and *Bioenergetics* (1975, Penguin/Arkana), ch. 5 \"Character Structure\", p.151 ff. https://archive.org/details/languageofbody00lowe\n3. IAHIP, \"A Brief Outline of the Characterology of Alexander Lowen\": https://iahip.org/page-1076503\n4. EuroPsyche / EAP, Bioenergetic Analysis modality description: https://www.europsyche.org/About/Modality-Representations/Psychotherapeutic-Approaches/Bioenergetic-Analysis\n5. May J., \"Empirical Analyses of the Character Typologies of Lowen and Kelley\", *USA Body Psychotherapy Journal* 5(2), 2006: https://www.ibpj.org/issues/usabpj-articles/(6)_May__J._Empirical_Analyses_of_the_Character_Typologies._USABPJ_5.2__2006.pdf\n6. Kessler S., *The 5 Personality Patterns* (2015) and official site: https://the5personalitypatterns.com/ \u00b7 https://the5personalitypatterns.com/how-to-discern-your-patterns/ \u00b7 https://courses.the5personalitypatterns.com/rels-course/ \u00b7 sample chapter https://www.the5personalitypatterns.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/SampleChapter.pdf\n7. Johnson S. M., *Character Styles* (1994, Norton): adds the \"right\" framing (right to exist, need, etc.).\n8. Kurtz R., *Body-Centered Psychotherapy: The Hakomi Method* (1990); Hakomi Institute: https://hakomiinstitute.com/about/the-hakomi-method\n9. Keleman S., *Emotional Anatomy* (1985) \u2014 shape-based alternative (rigid/dense/swollen/collapsed).\n\n## 1. Wilhelm Reich: character armour and the 7 segments\n- **Character armour** = the sum of chronic defensive attitudes; **muscular armour** = its bodily counterpart (chronic muscle holding that binds anxiety, rage and sexual excitation). Reich held them to be functionally identical: loosening one loosens the other [1].\n- Armour forms in **horizontal rings (segments)** roughly at right angles to the spine; energy/feeling \"streams\" along the body's long axis and is blocked where a ring is tight. Dissolve **from the top down**, eyes first, pelvis last [1].\n- Tools: breathing (especially full exhalation), direct pressure on tight muscles, expressive movement/sound, and character analysis of *how* the person speaks, not only what.\n\n| # | Segment | Key structures | Typical held emotion / function |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ocular | eyes, forehead, occiput, scalp | contact, seeing, fear (\"staring\" or \"far-away\" eyes) |\n| 2 | Oral | jaw, lips, chin, occiput base | crying, biting, sucking, rage |\n| 3 | Cervical | neck, throat, tongue | swallowing feelings, holding back sound |\n| 4 | Thoracic | chest, shoulders, arms, hands | longing, sobbing, rage of arms, heart feelings |\n| 5 | Diaphragmatic | diaphragm, solar plexus, lower ribs | the key breath block; anxiety |\n| 6 | Abdominal | belly muscles, lower back | fear of attack, \"gut\" feelings |\n| 7 | Pelvic | pelvic floor, hips, buttocks, adductors, legs | pleasure, sexuality, anger, grounding |\n\nThese match BBTRS's seven belts of tension (see 03); BBTRS adds pairings (jaw\u2194pelvis, throat\u2194diaphragm).\n\n## 2. Lowen's five character structures (Bioenergetics, 1975)\nLowen used psychiatric terms on purpose so they would be recognisable [2,3]. Most people are **mixes**; Lowen says one rarely sees a pure type. \"Gift\" and \"right\" columns come from later authors (Johnson [7], Kessler [6], BBTRS), not Lowen himself.\n\n| | Schizoid | Oral | Psychopathic | Masochistic | Rigid |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Developmental period (theory) | prenatal\u2013first months | nursing / early infancy | ~2\u20134 yrs, power struggle / seduction | ~1.5\u20133 yrs, control & autonomy | ~3\u20136 yrs, love/sexuality (oedipal) |\n| Core issue / right | existence (rejection) | need (deprivation) | trust (betrayal, being used) | autonomy (over-control, humiliation) | love & sexuality (rejection of heart/sexual feeling) |\n| Body shape | narrow, contracted, often thin; two halves can look different; joints tight; cold hands/feet | long, thin, under-developed; weak legs and arms, small pelvis; slumping | top-heavy, inflated chest/shoulders; narrow hips, weak/tight legs (or a second, seductive, flexible type) | short, stocky, heavily muscled; short thick neck & waist; pelvis tucked forward | well-proportioned, upright, straight back held stiff; head high |\n| Energy (Lowen) | withdrawn from periphery; frozen at core | undercharged, weak flow to periphery | displaced upward to head; lower body undercharged | strongly charged but tightly held, blocked at throat and waist | well charged at periphery but held by long muscles; heart separated |\n| Eyes | blank, far-away, \"not home\", fearful | soft, pleading, needy, may cry easily | watchful, piercing, controlling or seductive | suffering, confused, sometimes hidden | bright, alert, may lack softness |\n| Breath | very shallow, held in chest; freezes | shallow, weak inhale (\"can't take in\") | held in upper chest, diaphragm/waist band tight; little in belly | held, compressed; belly tension, sighing | controlled, chest held, limited exhale of feeling |\n| Posture / grounding | weight on outer feet, knees locked, poorly grounded | collapsed chest, knees locked to stay up, sinks | chest up, chin lifted, legs stiff, pelvis held | compressed, head forward/down, buttocks tucked | military: chest out, back arched or straight, pelvis tipped back |\n| Core fear | annihilation, being here at all | abandonment, not enough | being controlled, used, weak | being crushed, invaded, humiliated | losing control, surrender, being hurt in love |\n| Main defence | dissociation, leaving the body, intellect | dependency, clinging, or \"I need nothing\" | control, manipulation, being on top | endurance, passive resistance, spite inside | pride, performance, control of feeling |\n| Gifts (later authors) | creativity, spirituality, sensitivity | warmth, nurturing, empathy | leadership, courage, vision | loyalty, patience, endurance, humour | elegance, competence, drive, passion |\n| Therapy needs | safety, grounding, slow contact, feeling the body is safe; never push breath hard | support to stand on own feet, legs/grounding, permission to need, strengthen inhale | trust built without power games; ground the legs, soften the chest/diaphragm, invite vulnerability | space and no pressure; release throat & waist, express anger/assertion, autonomy | soften the heart, allow surrender & play, release back and pelvis, less performance |\n\n## 3. Stephen Kessler's 5 Personality Patterns\nKessler (trained in Bioenergetics and Hakomi-related work) recast Lowen's types as **safety strategies, not types**: what you do when distressed [6]. Most people have a **primary** pattern and one **backup** pattern as distress rises; a few have three.\n\n| Kessler pattern | Strategy when distressed | Energy movement / centre | Lowen | BBTRS | Right (Kessler/Johnson) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Leaving** | flee, leave the body (\"gotta get away\") | out and up, head-centred | Schizoid | Dreamer (Unwanted Child) | Existence |\n| **Merging** | connect, please, appease, seek rescue | toward others, heart-centred | Oral | Needy | Need |\n| **Enduring** | hide, hunker down, passive \"you can't make me\" | in and down, belly-centred | Masochistic | Endurer | Autonomy |\n| **Aggressive** | fight, dominate, charm-to-control | up and out, gets bigger | Psychopathic | Leader | Trust |\n| **Rigid** | perform, do it right, follow rules | chest & belly tense to dampen feeling, head-centred | Rigid | Perfectionist | Love & sexuality |\n\nPractical: Kessler recommends identifying the pattern by **watching behaviour and the body under mild stress**, not by matching traits \u2014 useful for the intake (ask \"When stressed, what do you do first?\").\n\n## 4. Hakomi (Ron Kurtz) \u2014 useful additions\n- Kurtz's character map (sensitive/withdrawn, dependent/endearing, self-reliant, tough/generous & charming/manipulative, burdened/enduring, expressive/clinging, industrious/over-focused) is a softened Lowen variant [8].\n- Principles worth using in the app's tone: **mindfulness, non-violence, organicity, unity, mind-body holism**; work by gentle **\"little experiments\"** in mindfulness rather than confrontation. Fits MER's \"no agenda\" and trauma pacing (07).\n\n## Where sources disagree / cautions\n- **Number of types:** Lowen 1958 had more (oral, masochistic, hysterical, phallic-narcissistic, passive-feminine, schizophrenic, schizoid); 1975 reduced to five [2,3]. EuroPsyche uses \"narcissistic\" instead of \"psychopathic\" [4]; Johnson uses \"narcissistic\" and adds \"symbiotic\". Kelley's Radix uses only three (fear/anger/pain blocking) [5].\n- **Ages** for each stage differ between authors; treat as theory.\n- **Evidence:** a review of 12 empirical studies found mixed support \u2014 some correlations between body types and traits, but inter-rater reliability of body typing was weak and Lowen's claim that all levels co-vary was challenged [5]. EuroPsyche notes the system rests on clinical observation and needs scientific proof [4]. \u2192 Report wording should say \"may suggest\", combine with intake answers, and never diagnose.\n- Kessler explicitly rejects \"being a type\" [6]; Lowen describes types. App text: \"you tend to use the \u2026 pattern\".\n- Lowen-era pathologising labels (e.g. skin colour, body hair claims) are not used in reports.\n\n\n# 09 \u00b7 Anatomy Trains: expanded research (paraphrased)\n\nExtends 05-anatomy-trains.md. Reports should describe lines as **a useful map**, not proven anatomy, for the lines with weak evidence.\n\n## Sources\n1. Myers T., *Anatomy Trains: Myofascial Meridians for Manual Therapists and Movement Professionals*, 4th ed., Elsevier 2020. Official site: https://www.anatomytrains.com/ ; BodyReading course: https://www.anatomytrains.com/product/bodyreading-visual-assessment-of-the-anatomy-trains-webinar-series/\n2. Myers, Structural analysis chapter (BodyReading): https://basicmedicalkey.com/structural-analysis/\n3. Wilke J., Krause F., Vogt L., Banzer W. \"What Is Evidence-Based About Myofascial Chains: A Systematic Review.\" *Arch Phys Med Rehabil* 2016;97(3):454-61. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26281953/ (PDF: https://www.anatomytrains.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/wilke-pdf.pdf)\n4. Krause F., Wilke J., Vogt L., Banzer W. \"Intermuscular force transmission along myofascial chains: a systematic review.\" *J Anat* 2016;228(6):910-8. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5341578/\n5. Stecco C., *Functional Atlas of the Human Fascial System* (2015) \u2014 alternative fascial-sequence model (Fascial Manipulation).\n6. ABMP, Myers \"BodyReading the Myofascial Meridians\": https://www.abmp.com/learn/course/bodyreading-myofascial-meridians-3-superficial-back-line\n\n## Evidence at a glance (Wilke 2016, dissection) [3]\n| Line | Verified transitions | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Superficial Back Line | 3/3 (14 studies) | strong |\n| Back Functional Line | 3/3 (8) | strong |\n| Front Functional Line | 2/2 (6) | strong |\n| Spiral Line | 5/9 (21) | moderate, partial |\n| Lateral Line | 2/5 (10) | moderate\u2013strong, partial |\n| Superficial Front Line | 0 (7) | **none** \u2014 the rectus femoris\u2192rectus abdominis link crosses the bony pelvis |\n| Deep Front Line, arm lines | not reviewed | \u2014 |\nForce transmission (Krause 2016) [4]: moderate evidence for all 3 SBL transitions (plantar fascia\u2194Achilles, hamstrings\u2194gastrocnemius, hamstrings\u2194sacrotuberous ligament/thoracolumbar fascia), 1 of 2 BFL (lat\u2194contralateral glute max via TLF); FFL only slight. In-vivo clinical relevance is still uncertain.\n\n## Line by line\n### Superficial Back Line (SBL)\n- **Track/stations:** toes plantar surface \u2192 plantar fascia & short toe flexors \u2192 calcaneus \u2192 gastrocnemius/Achilles \u2192 femoral condyles \u2192 hamstrings \u2192 ischial tuberosity \u2192 sacrotuberous lig. \u2192 sacrum \u2192 lumbosacral fascia/erector spinae \u2192 occiput \u2192 galea aponeurotica \u2192 frontal bone/brow ridge.\n- **Photo signs:** side: knees hyperextended, ankle more plantar-flexed, lumbar hyperlordosis or flat back, short sub-occipitals with chin poking/forward head; back: uneven calf bulk, heel valgus/varus.\n- **Complaints:** plantar fasciitis, Achilles tendinopathy, calf cramps, hamstring tightness/strain, low back pain, sub-occipital tension headaches, eye strain. Evidence: plantar fascia\u2013Achilles continuity is the best-documented link [3,4]; hamstring stretching can increase cervical range in some trials (remote effects, debated).\n- **Bodywork:** plantar fascia release, slow calf/hamstring myofascial spreading, sacrotuberous and erector work, sub-occipital release, scalp work. **Movement:** forward fold/roll-down, downward dog, ball rolling under the foot, eccentric heel drops.\n\n### Superficial Front Line (SFL)\n- **Track:** dorsal toes \u2192 toe extensors, tibialis anterior \u2192 patella/quadriceps (rectus femoris) \u2192 AIIS/pubis \u2192 rectus abdominis \u2192 5th rib \u2192 sternalis/sternochondral fascia \u2192 SCM \u2192 mastoid/scalp.\n- **Photo signs:** side: \"startle\" \u2014 head forward, chest pulled down, pubis-to-ribs shortening, anterior pelvic tilt with tight quads, knees locked.\n- **Complaints:** shin splints, anterior knee pain, hip flexor tightness, shallow chest breathing, neck pain (SCM), TMJ via neck.\n- **Bodywork:** tibialis/quad release, abdominal fascia, sternal and SCM work. **Movement:** back-bends, camel, bridge, lunge with arm reach.\n- **Disagreement:** no dissection evidence for continuity [3]; Myers treats it as functional (mechanical link through pelvis).\n\n### Lateral Line (LL)\n- **Track:** 1st/5th metatarsal base \u2192 peroneals \u2192 fibular head \u2192 ITB/TFL/glute max (ant.) \u2192 iliac crest \u2192 obliques \u2192 intercostals \u2192 splenius capitis/SCM \u2192 mastoid.\n- **Photo signs:** front/back: high shoulder or hip, side-bent trunk, ribcage shift, deep waist crease one side, ankle pronation/supination, knock-knee or bow-leg.\n- **Complaints:** lateral knee (ITB) pain, ankle sprains/instability, lateral hip/trochanteric pain, side-of-neck tension.\n- **Bodywork:** peroneal, ITB/TFL, QL and intercostal work, lateral neck. **Movement:** side bends, gate pose, triangle, single-leg balance.\n\n### Spiral Line (SPL)\n- **Track:** skull \u2192 splenius \u2192 opposite rhomboids \u2192 serratus anterior \u2192 external obliques \u2192 contralateral internal obliques \u2192 ASIS \u2192 TFL/ITB \u2192 tibialis anterior \u2192 peroneus longus (\"stirrup\") \u2192 biceps femoris \u2192 sacrotuberous lig. \u2192 erectors \u2192 skull.\n- **Photo signs:** head rotation/tilt, one shoulder forward, rib cage rotated on pelvis, one knee turning in, unequal arches, scapulae at different distances from spine.\n- **Complaints:** rotational back pain, scoliosis patterns, one-sided shoulder/neck pain, unilateral knee tracking issues.\n- **Bodywork:** rhomboid\u2013serratus balance, oblique work, ITB/peroneus stirrup. **Movement:** twists, spiral walking, cross-body reaches. Evidence: partial [3].\n\n### Deep Front Line (DFL)\n- **Track (lower):** deep plantar \u2192 tibialis posterior & long toe flexors \u2192 popliteus fascia \u2192 adductors \u2192 pelvic floor \u2192 psoas/iliacus \u2192 ALL/front of spine \u2192 diaphragm crura \u2192 pericardium/mediastinum \u2192 longus colli, scalenes \u2192 hyoid muscles \u2192 jaw/cranium.\n- **Photo signs:** side: anterior or posterior tilt, pelvis shifted forward of feet, ribcage back, flattened lumbar or hyperlordosis, forward head; front: collapsed inner arches, knee valgus; breath held high or in the belly only.\n- **Complaints:** groin/adductor strain, pelvic floor dysfunction, deep low back pain, psoas-related hip pain, breathing restriction, jaw tension, neck \"short scalenes\".\n- **Bodywork:** inner-arch & tibialis posterior, adductors, gentle psoas & diaphragm release, scalenes, floor of mouth. Breathwork is central here. **Movement:** diaphragmatic breathing, constructive rest, TRE, lunges with long inner leg.\n- **Not reviewed** in Wilke 2016; mostly Myers' own dissection and clinical reasoning.\n\n### Arm lines\n- **Superficial Front Arm:** pec major/lat \u2192 medial intermuscular septum \u2192 forearm flexors \u2192 palm. **Deep Front Arm:** pec minor/clavipectoral fascia \u2192 biceps \u2192 radial periosteum \u2192 thenar. **Superficial Back Arm:** trapezius \u2192 deltoid \u2192 lateral septum \u2192 extensors. **Deep Back Arm:** rhomboids/levator \u2192 rotator cuff \u2192 triceps \u2192 ulnar periosteum \u2192 hypothenar.\n- **Photo signs:** rounded/protracted shoulders (DFAL, pec minor), elevated shoulders (SBAL), backs of hands facing forward (internal rotation), winging, bent elbows.\n- **Complaints:** carpal tunnel-like symptoms, tennis/golfer's elbow, shoulder impingement, thoracic outlet.\n- **Bodywork/movement:** pec minor & clavipectoral release, forearm work; wall slides, doorway stretch, thread-the-needle.\n\n### Functional lines\n- **Back FL:** lat dorsi \u2192 TLF \u2192 sacral fascia \u2192 contralateral glute max \u2192 vastus lateralis \u2192 patellar tendon. **Front FL:** pec major lower edge \u2192 rectus sheath \u2192 pubic symphysis \u2192 contralateral adductor longus. **Ipsilateral FL:** lat \u2192 external oblique \u2192 sartorius.\n- **Photo signs:** mostly in movement; diagonal shoulder\u2013opposite-hip asymmetry, sport one-sidedness.\n- **Complaints:** sacroiliac pain, groin pain in kicking/throwing sports.\n- **Movement:** contralateral reaching, crawling, bird-dog, cross-crawl, gait work. Evidence: strong for continuity [3].\n\n## Where sources disagree\n- **Existence vs. relevance:** Wilke 2016 supports continuity for SBL/BFL/FFL but finds none for SFL [3]; continuity \u2260 proven force transfer or clinical effect [4].\n- **Myers vs. Stecco:** Stecco's Fascial Manipulation uses myofascial *sequences* and *centres of coordination*, a different map; overlapping but not identical [5].\n- **Myers vs. strict biomechanics:** critics note lines are chosen by direction/depth rules and may underplay bony attachments; Myers frames them as a map, \"not the territory\" [1].\n- **Posture\u2013pain link:** visual postural assessment has modest reliability and posture correlates weakly with pain in research; use photos as a conversation starter combined with the pain map, never as proof of cause.\n";
export const EXAMPLE = "{\"date\": \"26 Oct 2026\", \"plan\": [{\"n\": 1, \"home\": \"5 min slow breathing each evening; notice where anxiety sits\", \"focus\": \"Assessment, safety and resources; whole body\", \"bodywork\": \"Feet and hands, rocking; agree on pressure and consent (no touch preferences given); gentle sub-occipital hold\", \"breathwork\": \"Observe the natural breath; slow nose breathing with longer exhales\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"home\": \"Barefoot standing, feel the feet 2 min a day\", \"focus\": \"Grounding into the base you already have\", \"bodywork\": \"Feet, calves and legs, long strokes upward; sacrum hold\", \"breathwork\": \"Grounding breath into the feet and pelvis\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"home\": \"Humming 3 min; chin-tuck neck mobility\", \"focus\": \"Neck and occiput (Superficial Back Line top), cervical belt\", \"bodywork\": \"Sub-occipitals, back of neck C7 to occiput, scalp\", \"breathwork\": \"Humming and long exhales\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"home\": \"Jaw release with tongue rest; evening shoulder shrug-and-drop\", \"focus\": \"Front of the neck and jaw (Superficial Front Line)\", \"bodywork\": \"SCM and scalenes gently, masseter and temporalis, hyoid area\", \"breathwork\": \"Loose-jaw breath, soft sighs\"}, {\"n\": 5, \"home\": \"Doorway chest opener; arms hanging and shaking\", \"focus\": \"Shoulders and arm lines \\u00b7 photo re-assessment\", \"bodywork\": \"Upper trapezius, levator scapulae, pec minor, shoulder blades, arm lines\", \"breathwork\": \"Breath into the back ribs and shoulder blades\"}, {\"n\": 6, \"home\": \"Lying over a rolled towel along the spine, 5 min\", \"focus\": \"Thoracic belt and upper back\", \"bodywork\": \"Rib springing on the exhale, thoracic spine, intercostals, sternum\", \"breathwork\": \"Connected breath into chest and back, pressure released on the exhale\"}, {\"n\": 7, \"home\": \"Box or coherent breathing in FasciApp when anxiety rises\", \"focus\": \"Diaphragm and solar plexus (anxiety)\", \"bodywork\": \"Diaphragm attachments, solar plexus, upper belly with consent\", \"breathwork\": \"Belly and low-rib breath; pendulation between tension and ease\"}, {\"n\": 8, \"home\": \"Lean against a wall and let it hold you, 3 min\", \"focus\": \"Trust and support: letting the back be held\", \"bodywork\": \"Supported back work, holding the upper back and neck while you let the head be heavy\", \"breathwork\": \"Fuller connected breath, letting sound out\"}, {\"n\": 9, \"home\": \"Personal 10-min evening wind-down routine\", \"focus\": \"Integration: front and back lines from feet to head\", \"bodywork\": \"Long fascial strokes feet to scalp, unwinding of neck and shoulders\", \"breathwork\": \"Full connected breath session\"}, {\"n\": 10, \"home\": \"Ongoing plan with FasciApp practices\", \"focus\": \"Completion, re-assessment and self-care plan\", \"bodywork\": \"Whole-body integration, grounding holds; photo re-assessment\", \"breathwork\": \"Calm breath, closing meditation\"}], \"client\": \"Giten\", \"summary\": \"A lean, light, upright body that feels itself mostly in the upper body: the head and chin reach slightly forward, the shoulders carry the evening tension and anxiety, while the legs and core feel well grounded and strong.\", \"version\": 1, \"body_map\": {\"note\": \"Most charge sits in the neck and shoulders; legs and core look grounded and strong, a real resource to settle into.\", \"belts\": {\"oral\": 1, \"ocular\": 1, \"pelvic\": 0, \"cervical\": 3, \"thoracic\": 3, \"abdominal\": 0, \"diaphragmatic\": 2}, \"issues\": [{\"text\": \"Head and chin reach forward; the front neck muscles may tend to shorten.\", \"view\": \"front\", \"label\": \"Front of the neck\", \"region\": \"neck_front\"}, {\"text\": \"Possible evening tension and aching (4/10) in the top of the shoulders and upper trapezius.\", \"view\": \"both\", \"label\": \"Shoulders\", \"region\": \"shoulders\"}, {\"text\": \"The back of the neck may work hard to hold the head up and forward.\", \"view\": \"back\", \"label\": \"Base of the skull\", \"region\": \"neck_back\"}, {\"text\": \"A gently rounded upper back; possible tendency to carry load between the shoulder blades.\", \"view\": \"back\", \"label\": \"Upper back\", \"region\": \"upper_back\"}, {\"text\": \"Anxiety may show as a high, held breath. To check in session 1.\", \"view\": \"front\", \"label\": \"Breath / diaphragm\", \"region\": \"diaphragm\"}]}, \"red_flag\": {\"text\": \"No red flags were ticked and no health conditions or medication were reported. Shoulder pain is moderate (4/10, aching), mostly in the evening. Anxiety is named as a main concern: if it ever feels overwhelming, constant or affects sleep and daily life, please also talk with a doctor or therapist. If you notice numbness, tingling or weakness down the arms, see a doctor before deep neck and shoulder work.\", \"level\": \"none\"}, \"sections\": [{\"key\": \"map\", \"core\": \"A Psychopathic (Leader) pattern, the right to trust, with a light Masochistic (Endurer) backup, the right to autonomy: you experience yourself mostly in the upper body (your own words), the head and chin reach forward and the shoulders hold the load (character and body), the cervical and thoracic belts look most charged (belts), the Superficial Front and Back Lines and the arm lines may be pulling around the neck and shoulders (fascia), with anxiety as the emotional theme (emotion), in a nervous system that sleeps well but tightens towards evening (window of tolerance: fairly wide, with an evening edge).\", \"links\": [[\"Character\", \"Psychopathic (Leader) \\u00b7 The right to trust (primary) \\u00b7 Masochistic (Endurer) \\u00b7 The right to autonomy (light backup)\"], [\"Body signs\", \"Lean frame, head and chin forward, gently rounded upper back, shoulders slightly lifted; feet planted and even\"], [\"Energetics\", \"Charge felt in the upper body; well grounded, strong core, finishes projects\"], [\"Belts\", \"Cervical and thoracic over-charged; diaphragm to watch; pelvic/legs balanced\"], [\"Fascial lines\", \"Superficial Front Line (front of neck), Superficial Back Line (sub-occiput), arm lines\"], [\"Emotional theme\", \"Anxiety; staying in control and leading; the right to trust\"], [\"Nervous system\", \"Good sleep, low stated stress, anxiety and shoulder tension rising in the evening\"]], \"title\": \"Whole-being map\", \"subtitle\": \"How the layers meet in you\", \"direction\": \"From up and forward to down and back: let the head settle over the spine, drop the shoulders, and let the good grounding you already have carry the upper body so the evening anxiety has somewhere to land.\", \"strengths\": [\"A lean, light, upright frame (178 cm, 63 kg) with even feet and a stable stance\", \"Well grounded with a strong core, and the energy to finish what you start\", \"Good sleep and regular exercise three times a week at 55\"]}, {\"key\": \"character\", \"items\": [{\"h\": \"Psychopathic (Leader) \\u00b7 The right to trust\", \"p\": \"Primary pattern, medium-low confidence. Your answers lean clearly here (9/15, highest by far), especially leading, pushing or charming to get things done, staying in control, and finding it hard to show weakness. In Kessler's terms, the Aggressive safety strategy: under stress, energy moves up and forward to stay on top. Gift: vision, courage, leadership and charisma. Life task: to trust, to let others support you and to let energy come down from the head into the whole body. The body partly supports this: energy is felt in the upper body and the head and chin lead forward. It does not show the broad, inflated chest often described for this pattern; your frame is lean, so we hold this lightly.\"}, {\"h\": \"Masochistic (Endurer) \\u00b7 The right to autonomy\", \"p\": \"Light backup, low confidence (4/15). Gift: steadiness, patience and reliability. Life task: to say yes and no freely and let pressure out instead of holding it. Shoulders that carry tension, the 'finishes projects' marker and a strong core may echo a quieter enduring layer. Schizoid and Rigid both scored 3/15 and Oral 2/15: low, but the lean frame and the anxiety are worth keeping in view.\"}, {\"h\": \"A note\", \"p\": \"Following Steven Kessler (The 5 Personality Patterns), these are safety strategies we use under stress, usually one primary and one backup, not fixed types or labels. Your answers and your body don't fully agree here, which is normal: the sessions will show more than one set of photos.\"}], \"title\": \"Reichian character structure\", \"subtitle\": \"Reich \\u00b7 BBTRS \\u00b7 Kessler\\u2019s 5 Personality Patterns \\u00b7 gifts first\"}, {\"key\": \"body\", \"items\": [{\"h\": \"Positive qualities\", \"p\": \"An upright, light and lean body, feet planted evenly, arms hanging relaxed close to the trunk, and an open, alert face.\"}, {\"h\": \"Front\", \"p\": \"Head fairly centred; shoulders roughly level, perhaps a touch high towards the ears. Arms hang close and relaxed. Hips and knees look even and the feet point straight ahead with an even stance. Energy reads as light and evenly spread, a little gathered at the head and shoulders.\"}, {\"h\": \"Side\", \"p\": \"The head and chin reach forward of the shoulders and the upper back is gently rounded, while the pelvis and legs stand fairly straight under the trunk. This may suggest the neck and upper back working to hold the head up and out, a common seat of shoulder ache and evening tension.\"}, {\"h\": \"Back\", \"p\": \"Spine looks straight; shoulders roughly level, the right perhaps a fraction lower (right-handed). Back of the neck looks short and held. Legs and feet stand even with weight shared on both sides.\"}, {\"h\": \"Face and eyes\", \"p\": \"An open, attentive face with a steady, outward gaze; eyes look awake and engaged, which fits a forward, 'going towards the world' energy.\"}, {\"h\": \"Energetics and splits\", \"p\": \"You experience your body mainly in the upper body, while the practitioner markers show good grounding and a strong core. Possible gentle top/bottom and head/body split: alert head and shoulders over legs that are steady but may be less felt.\"}, {\"h\": \"Zones of fear (questions to explore)\", \"p\": \"Neck: what is hard to let go of, or to say? Shoulders: what responsibility am I carrying, and for whom? Upper back and heart: is it safe to be supported, to lean back and trust?\"}], \"title\": \"Body reading\", \"subtitle\": \"Face and body \\u00b7 front, side, back\"}, {\"key\": \"belts\", \"items\": [{\"h\": \"1 Ocular \\u00b7 balanced to watch\", \"p\": \"Eyes look awake and outward; anxiety may keep them alert. Right: to see.\"}, {\"h\": \"2 Oral \\u00b7 balanced to watch\", \"p\": \"Nothing reported; the forward chin is worth checking with the jaw. Right: to need and to be fed.\"}, {\"h\": \"3 Cervical \\u00b7 over-charged\", \"p\": \"Head and chin forward, short back of the neck, shoulder tension. Right: to be seen and heard.\"}, {\"h\": \"4 Thoracic \\u00b7 over-charged\", \"p\": \"Shoulder pain and tension (4/10, aching, evenings), gently rounded upper back. Right: to be loved.\"}, {\"h\": \"5 Diaphragmatic \\u00b7 to watch\", \"p\": \"No breath pattern reported; anxiety often lives here as a held or high breath. Right: to act. Pairs with the throat.\"}, {\"h\": \"6 Abdominal \\u00b7 balanced\", \"p\": \"Strong core marker; belly looks quiet. Right: to feel and want.\"}, {\"h\": \"7 Pelvic and legs \\u00b7 balanced\", \"p\": \"Well grounded, even stance, regular exercise. Right: to be here and to have.\"}], \"title\": \"Belts of tension\", \"subtitle\": \"Reich's seven belts\"}, {\"key\": \"fascia\", \"items\": [{\"h\": \"Superficial Front Line and front neck (release first)\", \"p\": \"With the head forward, the front of the neck (SCM, scalenes) and upper chest may shorten. Freeing them helps the head settle back over the trunk.\"}, {\"h\": \"Superficial Back Line (upper part)\", \"p\": \"Sub-occipitals and the back of the neck work to hold the head up; the upper back is pulled long and rounded. This station sits right under your shoulder ache.\"}, {\"h\": \"Arm lines\", \"p\": \"Upper trapezius, levator scapulae and pec minor link the neck to the shoulders and arms: likely carriers of the evening shoulder tension.\"}, {\"h\": \"Deep Front Line\", \"p\": \"Strong core and good grounding suggest this line is fairly well supported; we'll check the diaphragm and front of the neck as its upper stations if the anxiety shows in the breath.\"}], \"title\": \"Fascial lines\", \"subtitle\": \"Anatomy Trains view\"}, {\"key\": \"emotion\", \"items\": [{\"h\": \"Themes you shared\", \"p\": \"You named anxiety. In a Leader pattern this often sits behind staying in control and keeping going: a quiet worry about what happens if you let go or show weakness. Your answers about trust point the same way. We'll only explore what you choose to bring.\"}, {\"h\": \"Breath\", \"p\": \"No breath pattern was ticked, so we'll observe it in session 1. Anxiety with shoulder tension often goes with a high, quick breath; the invitation is to let the breath drop into the belly and back ribs with longer exhales.\"}, {\"h\": \"Nervous-system state\", \"p\": \"Good sleep and a low stress rating sit alongside anxiety and evening tension, which suggests a fairly wide window that narrows as the day goes on. Grounding and strong core are real resources, so the plan can build steadily while keeping a calm close to each session.\"}, {\"h\": \"Physical pattern\", \"p\": \"Tension that builds through the day into the shoulders, with a forward head; a lean body may have less padding, so pressure is agreed and adjusted together.\"}], \"title\": \"Emotional patterns\", \"subtitle\": \"Including nervous-system state\"}], \"disclaimer\": \"This assessment is a somatic and educational reflection based on your photos and answers, drawn from Myofascial Energetic Release, BioDynamic Breathwork and Anatomy Trains perspectives. It is not a medical diagnosis or treatment and doesn't replace a doctor, physiotherapist or psychotherapist. Body-type and emotional links are possibilities to explore, not labels. Please consult a medical professional about pain, injury or health conditions.\", \"photo_note\": \"Good, clear photos: full body including the feet in all three views, against a plain wall. The camera was a little close and low, which slightly exaggerates the head and feet; for re-assessment, stand 2\\u20133 m away with the camera at hip height, ideally in a fitted top so the shoulder blades and spine show.\"}";
